fix(attestation): refuse to sign a unit that did not succeed or wrote nothing

A delegation whose write was refused still produced a signed envelope reading
"artifacts":[],"verification":{"exitCode":0,"passed":true}, and wrote it as a git note. Every
field was true -- the verification command really did run and really did exit 0, against the
tree a previous run had already committed -- and the record was worthless: a green
verification over nothing, indistinguishable at the point of consumption from a verified
change.

The withheld invariant was aimed at the wrong condition. It asked whether a verification
process ran, which was true. It never asked whether the unit succeeded, or whether it
produced anything at all. Both are now asked, and each refuses rather than qualifying.

The delegation's status is resolved once, above the envelope, and feeds both the gate and
the verdict a caller reads, so the two cannot disagree about the same unit.

verification.passed is deliberately NOT a third gate, and the reason is recorded in the code
beside the other two. resolveDelegateStatus returns SUCCESS only when the unit's own result
held, which requires failed === 0, so the condition would be a second name for a fact the
status already carries -- and it would not have caught this, since the record that shipped
read passed: true. It would, however, leave the payload's passed field unable to ever be
false and its three failure-path tests unreachable, in exchange for no additional
protection.

Red-then-green: four new plan tests fail against the old gate and pass against the new one.
149 tests pass across 5 files. The dist gate and the README consistency gate are green.

Reaching this in the live delegation path needs a DSH restart: plugin options are read at
registration and the module is loaded once, so a rebuilt dist is not what a running host
executes.
